# Build context exclusions. Both Dockerfiles use the repo root as their context
# (they need `pnpm-workspace.yaml`, the lockfile, and `packages/contracts`), so
# everything below would otherwise be uploaded to the daemon on every build.
#
# The entries that matter for correctness, not just for speed:
#
#   node_modules/  — a macOS/arm64 pnpm store contains native binaries (Prisma's
#                    query engine, esbuild) built for the host. Copying them into
#                    a linux image and then installing on top produces a tree
#                    that is half host-built and fails at runtime, not at build.
#   dist/          — the image builds its own. A stale local `dist` copied in
#                    would be served instead, which is exactly the P37 failure
#                    mode (a plausible-looking `dist` that disagrees with source).
#   *.db           — the dev database. It must never reach a layer: the container
#                    keeps its data on a named volume at /data, and a baked-in
#                    file would both leak local data and be silently shadowed.
#   .env           — real local values; the container is configured by compose.

node_modules
**/node_modules

dist
**/dist
build
**/build
.turbo
**/.turbo
*.tsbuildinfo

.env
.env.*
!env.example
!**/env.example

*.db
*.db-journal
*.db-shm
*.db-wal
apps/api/prisma/data

.git
.gitignore
.github

coverage
**/coverage
playwright-report
test-results
blob-report
playwright/.cache
e2e

.DS_Store
.idea
.vscode
*.log

# Docs and editor config change on almost every commit and are not build inputs;
# excluding them keeps the layer cache from being invalidated by a typo fix.
docs
README.md
CLAUDE.md
instructions.md
.editorconfig
.prettierrc.json
.prettierignore
eslint.config.js
docker-compose.yml
.dockerignore
